    energy_reserve_coopt: { cell: "I", ev: "SPP-55 (2026-09-07, ZERO LP — the arm was killed at the pre-solve half of its pre-registered STOP gate; no screen, no control, nothing registered, keeper-3 UNCHANGED; PRECOMMIT-spp-55-2026-09-07.md, FINDING-spp-55-2026-09-07.md, docs/handoffs/spp55/): I — INERT. THE SPP ENTRY NOW EXISTS under this shared gate (model/reserves/spec.py::_spp_design, the MISO-RBDC template, NO new field): ONE BAA-wide Contingency Reserve family on SPP's OWN published Contingency Reserve Demand Curve (Protocols v119 §4.1.5(1)(a)/§4.1.5.2: scarcity factor 0.25/0.5/1.0 x ($1,000 + $100) = $275/$550/$1,100 per MW — corroborated to the dollar by the posted RTBM MCPs, which sit at exactly 275/550/1,100 in every 2023-2025 short interval; the charter's $250 spin VRL is a CAP on the spin sub-constraint and the $50,000 power-balance VRL is the energy slack's relaxation cap, neither a demand-curve step), requirement = 0.964 x 1.2 x hourly MSSC (RSG Operating Process 0820EXT00002 §4.1-4.3: largest single unit >= 600 MW x availability; Wolf Creek 1 -> 1,500 MW vs SPP's posted cleared 1,514/1,484 MW medians, model/measured 0.983/0.961). Six published constants, one MEASURED share, zero tuned. MEASURED FOOTPRINT: 140/117/109 five-minute short intervals in 68/45/37 events of median length ONE interval; 4/3/7 hour-long shortage hours; and 1/0/0 of the 42/59/68 C3c hours coincide with ANY reserve shortage (reserve-short hours price at a median $32-36). PRE-SOLVE GATE: keeper-3's reserve-eligible headroom is below the 1.5 GW requirement in 0/1/0 hours (min 2,884/1,169/2,399 MW; 3.4-17x the requirement inside the measured window) — the row can bind in NO measured shortage hour of any year (leg (i)(a) = 0.00, bar 0.50), so under rule 29 step 0 no LP was spent. STRUCTURAL READING: SPP's reserve scarcity is a 5-minute ramp/capacity object an hourly perfect-foresight LP without a deliverability bound clears out of 6-18 GW of slow-unit headroom (the miso-38 gate-4 lesson on SPP's own data); making the family LIVE is SPP-56's deliverability question (R-22), and C3c is a 5-minute RT price-formation object no hourly lever reaches (R-21). Re-test condition: a 10-minute ramp bound on the held MW that brings DELIVERABLE headroom below the requirement in the measured 4/3/7 hours (computable at zero LP with docs/handoffs/spp55/headroom.py) — never this family alone re-armed. PRIOR: SPP-21 seed U (no verdict minted). ANNOTATED by SPP-80 (2026-09-25, cell NOT moved -- stays I; ZERO LP): the 2019-2022 RTBM MCPs are now landed (data/raw/spp-or-mcp) and measured against SPP's 2022+ upper-tercile premium. Scarcity does NOT own the premium; it moved the wrong way. Upper-tercile hours with a scarcity-priced interval fell from 12.5/11.6/15.2 % (2019-21) to 4.7/3.4/3.1 % (2023-25), and the MEC uplift they carry fell from $3.3-4.9 to $1.8-2.4/MWh (ΔHR -0.7 to -0.9). Ramp product (2022-03-01) and uncertainty product (2023-07-06; first non-zero MCP 2023-10-09) upper-tercile MCPs average $3-5 and ~$0/MW. Evidence: docs/handoffs/FINDING-spp-80-upper-tercile-premium-2026-09-25.md section 1.2." },
    ercot_multiproduct_as: { cell: ".", ev: "SPP-21 (2026-09-06) shard seed, no verdict minted (docs/multi-iso/spp-addition-plan-2026-09.md §4 W5 queue / §5 row SPP-21) — n/a: the mechanism's object is ERCOT's own market/registry object and no non-owner ISO has entered this cell at HEAD, so there is no SPP object to arm. Not a verdict; a later lane that identifies an SPP analogue re-opens it as U." },
    online_capacity_envelope: { cell: "U", ev: "SPP-83 (2026-09-26, cell NOT moved -- stays U; ZERO LP; docs/handoffs/FINDING-spp-83-keeper-clears-on-offline-capacity-2026-09-26.md, probe scripts/probes/_spp83_keeper_online_vs_market.py): KEEPER SIDE of SPP-82 measured. On SPP-82's exact sample hours the keeper's available fleet MW exceed SPP's published ONLINE thermal capacity by +8.17/+8.04/+8.17/+8.85/+8.09/+6.90 GW (2019-2024), ~6 GW of it gas (keeper coal is BELOW SPP online coal every year); keeper headroom 12.2-14.2 GW vs SPP online headroom 4.1-5.4 GW, with generation matched within 0.9 GW. The LP does clear on capacity the market kept offline, but the excess is YEAR-FLAT, not the 8->15 GW doubling: a level defect, not the 2023+ step. Re-clear bound (excess removed from the keeper's >=$10 gas stack cheapest first): +7.9/+7.9/+14.3/+23.8/+7.0/+13.1 $/MWh, so the 2023/24-minus-2019/20 differential is +2.1 against a needed +8.3, and it overshoots 2019/20 and 2021 -- a commitment state sized to online capacity would be a BODY lever and cannot carry SPP-79's pairing. Commitment-cost source survey FAILED (no SPP per-class start-up/no-load dollars public; ASOM gives fuel-level TIMES only; uplift/make-whole are outcomes). Re-open only with an engine commitment-state design lane AND a year-differential driver. SPP-21 (2026-09-06) shard seed, no verdict minted (docs/multi-iso/spp-addition-plan-2026-09.md §4 W5 queue / §5 row SPP-21) — U: UNTESTED. SPP has no keeper, no solve and no measurement on this mechanism; rules 25 [R-ISO-SCOPE] / 28(d) mean no other ISO's verdict fills this cell. The lever queue is docs/mechanism-testing-matrix.md §5.7. SPP-82 (2026-09-25, cell NOT moved -- stays U; ZERO LP; docs/handoffs/FINDING-spp-82-offered-not-setting-2026-09-25.md): SPP's 2023-24 upper-tercile premium is OWNED by offline, available, UNCOMMITTED capacity. On SPP-81b's exact sample, the floor on offline >=$10 MW priced below MEC (B10 - online thermal capacity, SPP hourly-generation-capacity-by-fuel-type) rises 7.5/8.2 -> 15.8/15.6 GW (2019/20 -> 2023/24), with online thermal capacity flat (31.9/31.4 -> 30.7/31.2), online headroom +0.7 GW and thermal outage flat; the RTBM offer file lists every resource, online or not. The MEASURED-PIN route (LP online capacity = SPP's published online MW) is RULE-13 INADMISSIBLE: online capacity is the market's commitment OUTCOME. The admissible form is a commitment-state structure driven by measured per-class start-up/no-load/min-run costs, which SPP does not publish (SPP-73). Re-open only on such a source." },
